#import "../../lib/24xx.typ": *

#show: xx.with(
  title: "AX&X: Towns, Keeps, and Marches",
  subtitle: "A town to adventure from, and the stronghold you raise. A strongholds supplement for AX&X.",
  cols: 2,
)

= Ground You Hold

The dungeon is a place you raid and leave. A hold is a place you keep. It is where you stack what the dark gave up, mend between delves without the long walk back to town, and plant a banner the land must reckon with. This supplement raises one, from a night's camp to a walled stronghold, and runs the day the enemy comes to tear it down.

It is built from pieces the other books already carry. The garrison is a #k[Warband]; the patron who grants your charter and the raiders who covet your walls are #k[Powers of the Land]; the countdown the raids keep is the #k[Foretold Doom]; the season you spend raising a wall is #k[Downtime]; the hands that man it are the funnel's survivors and your hirelings. None of this is needed to crawl a dungeon. Bring it in when the party stops being wanderers and wants a place on the map.

= The Town and the Wilds

A party adventures out from a safe place and bears its winnings back to it. At the start that place is a #k[town], the farthest outpost of law before the wilds begin: a taproom to plan in, a market to outfit at, a temple to mend you, and men to hire. You do not own it, but you rest, resupply, and tally your treasure as experience there, then set out again. This runs that open, player-driven campaign the West Marches made its name on: the party chooses where in the wilds to dare, and the country, not a plot, decides what it finds.

- #item[The Wilds][Beyond the last field lies frontier, regions each with its own tone and hazards: known landmarks, rumored ruins, and ground no one has yet walked. The party picks where to go and the GM stocks what is there. The players make the adventures, and the land waits to be walked.]
- #item[The Farther Out, the Deadlier][Danger rises with the walking distance from town, not as the crow flies. The region #k[Tier] in Travel sets it, each region harder than the last, so a party reaches as far as it dares for as rich a haul. A prize beyond their strength is marked and left for when they are higher Level.]
- #item[Rumors][Every foray begins with a lead traded in the taproom: a landmark, a rumored ruin, a region to be first into. Rumors of the Frontier, below, is where the next expedition is chosen.]
- #item[No Fixed Party][Take whoever has gathered to go, and set out. The town, its rumors, the map drawn so far, and the holds raised are the campaign's memory, not any one party.]
- #item[Winning the Town][You cannot build in a town you do not own, so you earn its favor instead. A Favored power opens its market, its healers, and the men it has to hire, as Powers of the Land sets out. Win the town over, and it serves you like a hold you never had to raise.]

= The Three Rungs

A party that wants more than a borrowed town raises a hold of its own. It climbs a ladder, the way a warband earns its grades. Each rung is a real thing the party can point to, and each buys one more reason not to walk all the way home.

- #item[Camp][A night's safety, no more. Set at dusk with the gear on your backs, struck by dawn: a watch, a fire, a ring of stakes. It shelters a rest but mends nothing a bedroll would not, and the first raid scatters it. Free, and gone when you leave.]
- #item[Outpost][Ground #k[cleared, watered, defensible, and held], a bolt-hole with a door that bars. An Outpost is a #k[Safe Haven] of your own: a week's rest here restores HP and ends Injured as any town does, and the walk to it is shorter than the walk to town. It holds a #k[cache], store Salvage, loot, and gear behind a lock, and seats #k[one Downtime Action] in the field, so the party can mend, craft, or study without the march back. Raising one takes #k[Salvage] and a season, below.]
- #item[Stronghold][An Outpost behind a #k[wall], with buildings inside it. The wall turns a raid into a siege and gives the garrison hard cover. Each building is a function the party used to walk to town for: a forge, an infirmary, a shrine. A Stronghold is a Haven that works for you, and a prize worth an army's march to take.]

= Salvage

Gold is experience the moment it reaches town, and a stronghold priced in gold is a hole you pour your levels into. So you build with #k[Salvage], hauled out of ruins by the #k[load]: dressed stone, seasoned timber, iron fittings, a looted forge, a fallen hall's worth of good material carted off by the wagon.

- #item[The Find][A cleared #k[lair] yields #k[1 to 2 loads]; a #k[vault] or a great ruin, #k[3 to 5]. The GM sets it when the place is taken, by how much worked stone and metal it holds. Bare caves give none.]
- #item[Hauled, Not Sold][Salvage is bulky and worth little to anyone but you. It does not sell, and coin does not buy it. It fills a wagon or a Stable's #k[haulage] the way loot fills slots, so getting Salvage home is the same haul as bearing out treasure. A hold raised near its quarry is a hold raised faster.]
- #item[Spent, Not Banked][Salvage goes into walls and buildings and stays there. It is the hold itself, taking shape.]

= Raising the Hold

Building is #k[Downtime]. A #k[Build] action, one of a character's two in a haven week, puts a season's labor and a store of Salvage into the hold. It wants the Salvage in the cache, hands to swing the hammers, the hirelings, the funnel's mob, or a hired crew, and a season safe enough to work.

- #item[Outpost][Clear and hold the ground, then spend #k[3 loads] and a season. You must hold the ground first: a dungeon cleared to its roots, or land a patron grants, below.]
- #item[The Wall][#k[5 loads] turns an Outpost into a Stronghold and earns it hard cover against a raid. Raise it before the first building, or the building has nothing to stand behind.]
- #item[Buildings][#k[2 to 4 loads] each, one raised or advanced per Build action. A Stronghold holds as many as its walls have room for, the GM's call, often #k[the founder's CHA modifier plus one].]
- #item[The Cost in Time][A season at the hearth fills a mark of the #k[Decline], as Downtime warns. Building is that season. Raise your walls while the Doom still has slack, because every wall is time the dark gains.]

= The Buildings

Each building is a reason the party used to march to town, now inside the walls, and each maps to a rule already written. Raise the ones your table leans on.

- #item[Infirmary][Fills two #k[Mending] marks a week and tends the Injured, as the Infirmary in Downtime. A party that lost its Cleric can live on this.]
- #item[Shrine][A seat for a Priest: healing, Remove Curse, and Turn Undead at the altar, and a step of Standing with a faith that blesses it. Hostile ground for the undead that raid by night.]
- #item[Forge][Mend and sharpen gear for free, and, with rare ore or a maker's-mark from the #k[Hammerdeep Concord], upgrade a weapon or wake magic in steel. The Craft action runs it.]
- #item[Scriptorium][Copy scrolls into the book, research a question, learn a tongue: the Study action, with the texts to hand. A Mage's home between delves.]
- #item[Stable][Mounts and a #k[wagon], and the #k[haulage] to carry Salvage and loot home. The Stable is what makes a heavy haul pay, and what carries the next hold's stone.]
- #item[Cartographer][Maps of the country around. The first #k[Travel Check] of a journey that starts from the hold runs at #k[+2], or #k[+4] once the ground is well charted.]
- #item[Hall][A great room that seats the garrison and draws followers. Raise the cap on hirelings and henchmen the hold can keep, and give a Warband a place to muster and winter.]

= Carrying Off the Treasure

A hoard is no good to you in the dungeon. Treasure is heavy, as the core's slots and the equipment supplement's Overloading set out, and bearing it out past whatever still lives below is half the work of winning it.

- #item[Bearing It Out][Coin, plate, and Salvage fill slots like any load. What the party cannot carry in one trip it must cache, hide, or leave, and what is left may be gone, or guarded, when you return. A great hoard means several trips, hired bearers, or pack animals.]
- #item[Bearers and Beasts][Porters, a mule, or a wagon carry what the party cannot, but none will follow into a tight crawl, down a shaft, or through deep water. They wait at the dungeon's mouth or the edge of safe ground while the party ferries the treasure out to them. A Stable's wagon turns a good haul into a wagonload borne home.]
- #item[Camp or Withdraw][With the day's take at the mouth and more still below, the party must choose. Withdraw to the town or hold: safe rest, and a Downtime Action if the base is your own, but the approach is marched again and the dungeon will have restocked. Camp at the dungeon mouth: a quick return to cleared ground, but the night risks a wandering encounter at the region's Tier, and a camp overrun loses the treasure cached there.]
- #item[Back to Town][Treasure counts as experience only once it is safe in town, a gold piece to the point, as the core says, and Salvage becomes stone for the walls. The foray is done, and the next sets out from a base the richer for it.]

= The Garrison

Walls do not fight; soldiers do. A hold is held by a #k[Warband], mustered and run by that supplement, standing watch while the party is in the field.

- #item[Who Mans It][The funnel's survivors, men-at-arms hired by the day, a levy a #k[Favored] power lends you, or henchmen too green to delve. Five or more under a banner is a warband, as ever.]
- #item[The Castellan][Someone holds it while you delve: a #k[henchman] Commander, or a #k[retired veteran] who has hung up their delving boots. A mustered-out character remains. Their gear and one knack or special ability stay with the company as the castellan's, a trainer, a quartermaster, or a captain of the watch, seeding the garrison they now command.]
- #item[Upkeep][Wages are coin, as the core hirelings rule, a man-at-arms at #k[5 GP a day]. A season's garrison is a real purse, and the first drain a hold puts on the party. A hold with a Stable and worked land feeds itself and pays part of its own keep, the GM's call.]

= The Raid

The hold has value, so the land comes for it. At each #k[Decline] step the Foretold Doom turns an enemy toward your walls, and a #k[Sign] can telegraph it a tick before. Who comes is a Hostile power, the Doom's own agent, or a beast lord whose ground you took.

- #item[The Raiders][Stat them as a #k[Warband] at the region's tier: a tame frontier sends a raiding file or two, a fallen heartland sends a legion. The deeper and darker the country, the heavier the host.]
- #item[Home, You Hold the Walls][If the party is there, run it as a #k[Warband] fight from fortified ground. A #k[Stronghold wall] is hard cover, the garrison strikes from behind it, and the raiders must breach or scale before their numbers tell. You can spend a Commander's Focus and the high ground.]
- #item[Away, One Roll at the Gate][If the party is delving when the raid lands, the castellan holds alone. Make one #k[defense roll]: the castellan's Command check, #k[1d20 + CHA + Level], or the garrison's #k[Warband HD] against the raiders', 12+. The wall adds #k[+2], a full garrison its weight. Success holds the gate for the loss of a file or a season's stores. Failure sacks something: roll on #k[At the Gates], below.]
- #item[After][A raid beaten earns Standing with the powers it threatened and the Spoils of a Warband field. A raid lost costs Salvage, a building, or a Haven, and the Doom reads the fall as its own.]

= Charter and Claim

Land has owners, even the wild. How you come to hold ground sets who your first enemy is.

- #item[By the Sword][Clear a dungeon to its roots and you hold the ground above it by right of the last one standing. Expect the thing you evicted, or its kin, to want it back.]
- #item[By Grant][A #k[Favored] or #k[Sworn] power deeds you land and a charter, and lends a levy to hold it. Now they share the hold with you, a forward post in their war, and its enemies are the ones they name.]
- #item[By Taking It][Seize a standing keep, a bandit hold, or an abandoned tower, and inherit its walls and its grudges both. Faster than building, but the old claim stands against you until someone settles it.]

#rolltable("1d8", "At the Gates", note: [Who comes for the hold, or what a lost defense costs. Roll when the Doom turns an enemy toward your walls.], (
  item[A Raiding File][A warband or two of the region's tier, after loot and gone by dawn. Beat it and the frontier learns your name.],
  item[A Siege][A host sits down to starve you out. A Count fills as the stores run; a sally or a relief force ends it.],
  item[The Old Tenant][The thing you evicted, or its heir, come to take back its ground. It knows the halls better than you do.],
  item[A Rival's Claim][A Hostile power disputes your charter, by a warband, a writ, or a bribed magistrate. Standing decides how it opens.],
  item[Betrayal Within][A file, a hireling, or the castellan is bought. The gate opens from inside unless a Loyalty check catches it.],
  item[The Doom's Agent][The enemy of the Foretold Doom marches on you by name. Heavier than the tier, and it does not deal.],
  item[Sabotage][No assault, but a fire in the stores, a fouled well, a cracked wall. You lose Salvage or a building, not blood.],
  item[Tribute Demanded][Pay in coin, Salvage, or a service, or be raided. Powers of the Land sets who asks and what it buys.],
))

#rolltable("1d6", "The Hold Throws Off", note: [What a thriving hold gives back between raids. Roll or pick when a season passes quietly.], (
  item[Recruits][Volunteers come to the banner: 2d6 x five militia, or a henchman worth keeping.],
  item[Tribute][The country around pays to be protected: 1d6 x 100 GP, or a wagon of Salvage.],
  item[A Rumor][Travelers bring word of a dungeon's door, a rival's move, or a lead the party can chase.],
  item[Standing][Word spreads of a hold well held: a step of Standing with a power that approves.],
  item[A Crafter][A smith, a sage, or a healer takes up residence, and a building runs the better for it.],
  item[A Claimant][Someone arrives with a prior claim, a cause, or a debt. Trouble, or an ally, by how you meet it.],
))

#colbreak()

= Running a Hold

- #item[Start at the Outpost][Do not sketch a castle on day one. The first real hold is one cleared dungeon, three loads, and a door that bars. The ladder earns the rest.]
- #item[Let the Doom Pace It][Build in the slack seasons, hold in the hard ones. A hold is the party betting that what they raise outlasts what the Doom sends. Sometimes it does not.]
- #item[Hand It to a Player][One player keeps the hold the way another keeps the warband: its Salvage, its garrison, its buildings, its castellan. A page of your own tracks it.]

#rolltable("1d8", "Rumors of the Frontier", note: [A lead traded in town, and where to set out next. Pitch the Tier to how far out it lies.], (
  item[A Barrow Opened][A storm has breached an old tomb and its wards are failing. The dead, and what they were buried with.],
  item[The Flooded Mine][A drowned dwarf working, abandoned to its deep veins and whatever moved in after.],
  item[A Dead Mage's Tower][Half-standing and trapped, with books, workings, and worse still inside.],
  item[The Beast's Lair][A predator or monster dens near a road, over a season's worth of stolen hoard.],
  item[A Darkened Shrine][A holy place gone silent. Its treasures remain, and so does the reason it fell.],
  item[The Sunken Keep][A fallen stronghold: Salvage there for the hauling, and its old garrison risen to guard it.],
  item[An Unmapped Cave][A dark the locals shun. The Tier is unknown until you are inside it.],
  item[Deeper In][A way down from ground you already cleared, to older and richer dark.],
))

// Hold Record: a print worksheet, one per hold. The reflowable HTML export
// drops its form spacing and boxes, so the whole page is built into the PDF
// only. The context guard below omits it from the HTML "read" view.
#let rl = 0.6pt + luma(45%)
#let blank(w) = box(width: w, height: 0.95em, stroke: (bottom: rl))
#let lbl(s) = text(font: heading-font, weight: "bold", size: 7.5pt, upper(s))
#let field(label, w) = [#lbl(label + ":")#h(0.3em)#blank(w)]
#let sq = box(width: 8pt, height: 8pt, stroke: 0.5pt)
#let check(s) = [#box(width: 8pt, height: 8pt, stroke: 0.5pt)#h(0.3em)#s]
#let sechd(s) = block(above: 1.15em, below: 0.4em, breakable: false)[
  #text(font: section-font, size: 11pt, tracking: 0.04em, upper(s))
  #v(-0.2em)
  #line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.8pt + black)
]

#let hold-record = [
#pagebreak()
#set page(columns: 1)
#set par(leading: 0.5em, spacing: 0.55em)

#text(font: title-font, size: 22pt, tracking: 0.02em)[AX&X]#h(0.4em)#text(font: section-font, size: 12pt, tracking: 0.06em)[HOLD RECORD]
#h(1fr)#text(size: 7.5pt, fill: luma(40%))[Copy one per hold. The supplement runs each line.]
#v(0.15em)
#line(length: 100%, stroke: 1pt + black)
#v(0.4em)

#field("Name", 46%)#h(1.5em)#field("Region", 34%)
#v(0.8em)
#lbl("Rung")#h(0.6em)#check("Camp")#h(1.5em)#check("Outpost")#h(1.5em)#check("Stronghold")#h(3em)#lbl("Wall")#h(0.6em)#check("raised, +2 defense")
#v(0.8em)
#lbl("Charter")#h(0.6em)#check("By the Sword")#h(1.5em)#check("By Grant")#h(1.5em)#check("By Taking It")#h(3em)#field("Patron / Standing", 30%)

#sechd("Salvage in the Cache")
#text(size: 7.5pt, fill: luma(40%))[Loads hauled home. Outpost costs 3, the Wall 5, each building 2 to 4. Shade a box as you win it, cross it as you spend it.]
#v(0.35em)
#box(width: 100%)[#for i in range(24) [#sq#h(6pt)]]

#sechd("Buildings")
#text(size: 7.5pt, fill: luma(40%))[Check one as it is raised. Each maps to a rule the party walked to town for.]
#v(0.35em)
#check("Infirmary")#h(2em)#check("Shrine")#h(2em)#check("Forge")#h(2em)#check("Scriptorium")
#v(0.6em)
#check("Stable")#h(2em)#check("Cartographer")#h(2em)#check("Hall")#h(2em)#check(blank(24%))

#sechd("The Garrison")
#field("Castellan", 42%)#h(1.5em)#field("Command, CHA + Level", 30%)
#v(0.7em)
#field("Member: HD / HP / AC / Atk / die", 64%)
#v(0.7em)
#field("Keywords", 42%)#h(1.5em)#field("Upkeep / season", 26%)
#v(0.8em)
#lbl("Files")#h(0.6em)#box(width: 100%)[#for i in range(24) [#sq#h(6pt)]]
#v(0.4em)
#text(size: 7.5pt, fill: luma(40%))[HD = files x member HD. HP = 5 x HD. Attack +HD. Damage die +HD. The Warband book runs the fight.]

#sechd("Raids and the Doom")
#text(size: 7.5pt, fill: luma(40%))[A raid lands on each Decline step. Home is a warband fight from the walls; away is one defense roll, Command or garrison HD against the raiders', 12+, wall +2. Note what came and what it cost.]
#v(0.35em)
#for i in range(5) [#blank(100%)#v(0.85em)]

#sechd("Notes")
#for i in range(6) [#blank(100%)#v(0.85em)]
]

// Print only: the worksheet is omitted from the reflowable HTML export.
#context if target() != "html" {
  hold-record
}
